<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-directional-derivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Directional Derivatives and Gradient Vectors</title>

<p>
Recall that the partial derivatives <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> correspond
to the rate of change of <m>z = f(x,y)</m> in the <m>\mathbf i</m> and
<m>\mathbf j</m> directions. In this section, we generalize this notion
to the rate of change of <m>z = f(x,y)</m> in an arbitrary direction
<m>\mathbf u</m>, which is a unit vector of the form
<m>\mathbf u = u_1\mathbf i + u_2\mathbf j</m>.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-dirderiv-definition">
<title>The Definition of the Directional Derivative</title>

<definition xml:id="def-directional-derivative">
<title>Directional Derivative</title>
<statement>
<p>
The <term>derivative of <m>f</m> at <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> in the direction
of the unit vector <m>\mathbf u = u_1\mathbf i + u_2\mathbf j</m></term>
is the number
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-dirderiv-definition">
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\mathbf u,\,P_0}
= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{f(x_0 + su_1,\; y_0 + su_2) - f(x_0,y_0)}{s},
</men>

<p>
provided the limit exists. It can alternatively be denoted by
<m>\left(D_{\mathbf u} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
The geometry behind this definition is shown in
<xref ref="fig-dirderiv-setup-3d"/> and
<xref ref="fig-dirderiv-setup-prefigure"/>, side by side. Starting at the
point <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m>, we move a distance <m>s</m> in the direction
of the unit vector <m>\mathbf u</m> to reach the point
<m>P(x_0 + su_1,\, y_0 + su_2)</m>, and we compare the values
<m>f(x_0,y_0)</m> and <m>f(x_0 + su_1,\, y_0 + su_2)</m> of the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m> above the two points. Note that as <m>s \to 0</m>, the
two points <m>P_0</m> and <m>P</m> get closer and closer to each other.
</p>

<sidebyside widths="60% 37%" valign="middle">

<figure xml:id="fig-dirderiv-setup-3d">
<caption>
Moving from <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> the distance <m>s</m> in the direction
<m>\mathbf u</m> to reach <m>P(x_0 + su_1,\, y_0 + su_2)</m>. The
directional derivative compares the values <m>f(x_0,y_0)</m> and
<m>f(x_0 + su_1,\, y_0 + su_2)</m> on the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> as
<m>s \to 0</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-dirderiv-setup-3d" width="100%">
<shortdescription>
The displacement from P0 to P in the xy-plane and the corresponding
values of f on the surface above.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A three-dimensional view. In the <m>xy</m>-plane, a short thick arrow
at <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> shows the unit vector <m>\mathbf u</m>, and a
longer arrow from <m>P_0</m> to <m>P(x_0+su_1,\,y_0+su_2)</m> shows
the displacement <m>s\mathbf u</m>, with dashed segments for its
components <m>su_1\mathbf i</m> and <m>su_2\mathbf j</m>. Above the
plane floats a translucent blue surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m>. Dashed
vertical lines rise from <m>P_0</m> and <m>P</m> to the surface, where
the values <m>f(x_0,y_0)</m> and <m>f(x_0+su_1,\,y_0+su_2)</m> are
marked.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
