<interactive xml:id="interactive-dirderiv-definition" iframe="dirderiv-definition.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider for the angle to choose the unit vector <m>\mathbf u</m>,
and drag the slider for <m>s</m> to move the point <m>P</m> along the
direction <m>\mathbf u</m> starting at <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, for the function
<m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>. The readout compares the difference
quotient <m>\big(f(P) - f(P_0)\big)/s</m> with its limit as
<m>s \to 0</m>, which is the directional derivative
<m>\left(D_{\mathbf u} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-dirderiv-example-definition">
<title>Computing a Directional Derivative Using the Definition</title>

<example xml:id="example-dirderiv-definition">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, the point
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>, and the unit vector
<m>\mathbf u = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf i + \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf j</m>.
Use the definition of directional derivative to compute
<m>\left(D_{\mathbf u} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
We substitute <m>x_0 = y_0 = 1</m> and <m>u_1 = u_2 = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}</m>
in the definition:
</p>

<md xml:id="eq-dirderiv-example-limit">
<mrow>
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\mathbf u,P_0}
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{f(x_0 + su_1,\, y_0 + su_2) - f(x_0,y_0)}{s}
= \lim_{s\to 0}
\frac{f\!\left(1 + \frac{s}{\sqrt 2},\, 1 + \frac{s}{\sqrt 2}\right) - f(1,1)}{s}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}
\frac{1 + \left(1 + \frac{s}{\sqrt 2}\right)^2
+ \left(1 + \frac{s}{\sqrt 2}\right)^2 - 3}{s}
= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{\frac{4s}{\sqrt 2} + s^2}{s}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\left(\frac{4}{\sqrt 2} + s\right) = 2\sqrt 2.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The computation has a useful geometric picture, illustrated in the
interactive 3D figure below. Along the ray through
<m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the direction <m>\mathbf u</m>, the values of <m>f</m>
are the single-variable function
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-dirderiv-along-ray">
g(s) = f\!\left(1 + \tfrac{s}{\sqrt 2},\, 1 + \tfrac{s}{\sqrt 2}\right)
= 3 + 2\sqrt 2\, s + s^2,
</men>

<p>
and the difference quotient in the limit above is the slope of the
secant line of <m>g</m> through <m>s = 0</m> and <m>s</m>. As
<m>s \to 0</m> the secant lines approach the tangent line at
<m>s = 0</m>, whose slope is
<m>g'(0) = 2\sqrt 2 = \left(D_{\mathbf u} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>
</solution>

<interactive xml:id="interactive-dirderiv-secant-3d" iframe="dirderiv-secant-3d.html" width="100%" aspect="8:6">
<instructions>
<p>
The surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> is cut by the vertical plane through
<m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the direction <m>\mathbf u</m>. Drag the slider to
change <m>s</m>: the point <m>P</m> slides along <m>\mathbf u</m> in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, and the secant line through the surface points
<m>(1,\,1,\,f(P_0))</m> and <m>(1+su_1,\,1+su_2,\,f(P))</m> rotates
toward the tangent line. Press <q>s <m>\to</m> 0</q> to animate the
limit; the readout shows the secant slope <m>2\sqrt 2 + s</m>
approaching the directional derivative <m>2\sqrt 2</m>. Drag the figure
to view it from a different angle, or press the <q>Rotate</q> button.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-gradient-vector">
<title>The Gradient Vector</title>

<p>
Note that we can write the equation of the line through the point
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> with the direction
<m>\mathbf u = u_1\mathbf i + u_2\mathbf j</m> as
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-line-through-P0">
x(s) = x_0 + su_1, \qquad y(s) = y_0 + su_2.
</men>

<p>
We can use the above relation between <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>s</m>
and the chain rule, whose dependency diagram is shown in
<xref ref="fig-chain-rule-tree-tikz"/> and
<xref ref="fig-chain-rule-tree-prefigure"/>, to compute the directional
derivative as follows:
</p>

<md xml:id="eq-dirderiv-chain-rule">
<mrow>
\left(D_{\mathbf u} f\right)_{P_0}
= \left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\mathbf u,P_0}
\amp= \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{P_0}\frac{dx}{ds}
+ \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{P_0}\frac{dy}{ds}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{P_0}\cdot u_1
+ \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{P_0}\cdot u_2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \left[\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{P_0}\mathbf i
+ \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{P_0}\mathbf j\right]
\cdot \left(u_1\mathbf i + u_2\mathbf j\right).
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The vector in the first set of parentheses is known as the
<term>gradient vector</term>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-gradient-vector">
<title>Gradient Vector</title>
<statement>
<p>
The <term>gradient vector</term> of the function <m>f(x,y)</m> at
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> is the vector
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-gradient-definition">
\left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}
= \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{P_0}\mathbf i
+ \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{P_0}\mathbf j.
</men>

<p>
The gradient vector and the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y)</m> at
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> in the direction
<m>\mathbf u = u_1\mathbf i + u_2\mathbf j</m> are related via the
following dot product:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-dirderiv-gradient-relation">
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\mathbf u,\,P_0}
= \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}\cdot\mathbf u.
</men>
</statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig-chain-rule-tree-tikz">
<caption>
The dependency diagram for computing the directional derivative with
the chain rule: <m>z = f(x,y)</m> depends on <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>,
each of which depends on the arc length parameter <m>s</m> along the
line through <m>P_0</m> in the direction <m>\mathbf u</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-chain-rule-tree-tikz" width="45%">
<shortdescription>
The chain rule dependency tree from z equals f of x comma y down to s.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A tree diagram. At the top is <m>z = f(x,y)</m>. Two arrows labeled
<m>\partial f/\partial x</m> and <m>\partial f/\partial y</m> lead
down to the nodes <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. From <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>,
two arrows labeled <m>dx/ds</m> and <m>dy/ds</m> lead down to the
single node <m>s</m>.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round, scale=1.15]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \node (z) at (0,3)    {$z=f(x,y)$};
  \node (x) at (-1.6,1.5) {$x$};
  \node (y) at (1.6,1.5)  {$y$};
  \node (s) at (0,0)    {$s$};
  \draw[-{Latex}, oiblue, line width=1.1pt] (z) -- (x)
    node[midway, above left=-2pt] {$\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}$};
  \draw[-{Latex}, oiblue, line width=1.1pt] (z) -- (y)
    node[midway, above right=-2pt] {$\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}$};
  \draw[-{Latex}, oivermillion, line width=1.1pt] (x) -- (s)
    node[midway, below left=-2pt] {$\dfrac{dx}{ds}$};
  \draw[-{Latex}, oivermillion, line width=1.1pt] (y) -- (s)
    node[midway, below right=-2pt] {$\dfrac{dy}{ds}$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-chain-rule-tree-prefigure">
<caption>
The chain rule dependency diagram: multiplying down each branch and
adding the two products gives
