</image>
</figure>

<interactive xml:id="interactive-dirderiv-surface-slice" iframe="dirderiv-surface-slice.html" width="100%" aspect="8:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider to rotate the direction
<m>\mathbf u = \langle\cos\varphi, \sin\varphi\rangle</m> at the point
<m>(1,1)</m>. The vertical plane through <m>(1,1)</m> in the direction
<m>\mathbf u</m> cuts the surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> in a curve,
and the tangent line to this curve at <m>(1,1,3)</m> has slope
<m>D_{\mathbf u} f = \left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}\cdot\mathbf u</m>,
displayed above the figure. Use the buttons to snap <m>\mathbf u</m> to
the direction of <m>\nabla f</m>, its opposite, or a direction of zero
change. Drag the figure to view it from a different angle, or press the
<q>Rotate</q> button to spin it automatically.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-dirderiv-properties">
<title>More on the Directional Derivative</title>

<p>
The directional derivative can be expressed as
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-dirderiv-cos-theta">
D_{\mathbf u} f = \nabla f\cdot\mathbf u
= |\nabla f|\,|\mathbf u|\cos\theta = |\nabla f|\cos\theta,
</men>

<p>
where <m>\theta</m> is the angle between <m>\nabla f</m> and the unit
vector <m>\mathbf u</m>. This formula has three important consequences:
</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
When <m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\mathbf u</m> are in the same direction
(<m>\cos\theta = 1</m>), <m>f</m> increases most rapidly and
<m>D_{\mathbf u} f = |\nabla f|</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
When <m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\mathbf u</m> have opposite directions
(<m>\cos\theta = -1</m>), <m>f</m> decreases most rapidly and
<m>D_{\mathbf u} f = -|\nabla f|</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
When <m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\mathbf u</m> are orthogonal
(<m>\cos\theta = 0</m>), <m>f</m> does not change, and
<m>D_{\mathbf u} f = 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-dirderiv-angle-prefigure">
<caption>
Directions around <m>P_0</m>: <m>f</m> increases fastest along
<m>\nabla f</m>, decreases fastest along <m>-\nabla f</m>, and does not
change in the two directions orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m>.
</caption>
<image width="50%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-dirderiv-angle">
<diagram dimensions="(320,320)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.9,-1.9,1.9,1.9)">
<circle at="an-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="gray"
        dash="6 4" fill="none"/>
<vector at="an-grad" v="(1.405,0.655)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="red"
        thickness="3"/>
<vector at="an-u" v="(0.087,0.996)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"
        thickness="3"/>
<vector at="an-zero1" v="(-0.423,0.906)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="gray"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="an-zero2" v="(0.423,-0.906)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="gray"
        thickness="2"/>
<point at="an-P0" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>P_0</m>
</point>
<label at="an-grad-label" anchor="(1.5,0.8)" alignment="ne" color="red">
<m>\nabla f</m>
</label>
<label at="an-u-label" anchor="(0.09,1.1)" alignment="n" color="blue">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="an-zero1-label" anchor="(-0.55,1.15)" alignment="nw">
<m>D_{\mathbf u}f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="an-zero2-label" anchor="(0.55,-1.15)" alignment="se">
<m>D_{\mathbf u}f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="an-formula" anchor="(0,-1.62)" alignment="c">
<m>D_{\mathbf u} f = |\nabla f|\cos\theta</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A point P 0 surrounded by a dashed unit circle of possible directions. A long red arrow shows the gradient of f. A blue unit arrow shows a direction u making an angle theta with the gradient. Two gray arrows orthogonal to the gradient mark the directions of zero change. Below the circle is the formula: the directional derivative equals the magnitude of the gradient times cosine theta.">
  <annotation ref="an-grad" text="The gradient vector of f at P 0, drawn in red. Moving in this direction gives the most rapid increase, with directional derivative equal to the magnitude of the gradient."/>
  <annotation ref="an-u" text="A unit vector u, drawn in blue, making an angle theta with the gradient. Its directional derivative is the magnitude of the gradient times cosine theta."/>
  <annotation ref="an-zero1" text="A direction orthogonal to the gradient, where cosine theta is zero, so the directional derivative is zero and f does not change."/>
  <annotation ref="an-zero2" text="The opposite orthogonal direction, where the directional derivative is also zero."/>
  <annotation ref="an-circle" text="The dashed unit circle of all possible directions u at the point P 0."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<interactive xml:id="interactive-gradient-direction-angle" iframe="gradient-direction-angle.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the blue unit vector <m>\mathbf u</m> around the circle of
directions and watch the meter display
<m>D_{\mathbf u} f = |\nabla f|\cos\theta</m>. Use the buttons to snap
<m>\mathbf u</m> to the three special cases: the same direction as
<m>\nabla f</m> (fastest increase), the opposite direction (fastest
decrease), and a direction orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m> (no change).
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-gradient-directions-example">
<title>Directions of Fastest Increase, Fastest Decrease, and No Change</title>

<example xml:id="example-gradient-directions">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{x^2}{2} + \dfrac{y^2}{2}</m>. Find the directions in
which, at the point <m>P_0(1,1)</m>,
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> increases most rapidly,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> decreases most rapidly,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
there is no change in <m>f</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
<em>A.</em> As explained above, <m>f</m> increases most rapidly in the
direction of <m>\nabla f</m>. We have
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-directions-gradient">
\nabla f = x\,\mathbf i + y\,\mathbf j
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} = \mathbf i + \mathbf j.
</men>

<p>
Note that we need to divide
<m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} = \mathbf i + \mathbf j</m> by its
length to find the unit vector corresponding to the direction of most
rapid increase:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-directions-increase">
\frac{\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}}{\left|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}\right|}
= \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf i + \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf j.
</men>

<p>
<em>B.</em> For the direction of most rapid decrease, we take the
opposite direction:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-directions-decrease">
-\frac{\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}}{\left|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}\right|}
= -\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf i - \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf j.
</men>

<p>
<em>C.</em> To find the directions of zero change in <m>f</m>, we require
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-directions-zero-dot">
\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}\cdot\mathbf u
= (\mathbf i + \mathbf j)\cdot(u_1\mathbf i + u_2\mathbf j)
= u_1 + u_2 = 0
\quad\text{or}\quad u_1 = -u_2,
</men>

<p>
and since <m>\mathbf u</m> is a unit vector, we have
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-directions-unit-length">
|\mathbf u|^2 = u_1^2 + u_2^2 = 1.
</men>

<p>
After substituting <xref ref="eq-directions-zero-dot"/> in
<xref ref="eq-directions-unit-length"/>, we conclude
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-directions-zero-solve">
2u_1^2 = 1 \quad\Rightarrow\quad u_1 = \pm\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}.
</men>

<p>
Hence, the directions of zero change in <m>f</m> at <m>P_0(1,1)</m> are
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-directions-zero-answer">
\mathbf u = \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf i - \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf j
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\tilde{\mathbf u} = -\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf i + \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf j.
</men>

<p>
Note that <m>\tilde{\mathbf u} = -\mathbf u</m>. All four directions are
shown in <xref ref="fig-gradient-directions-tikz"/> and
<xref ref="fig-gradient-directions-prefigure"/>: the directions of zero
change are tangent to the level curve of <m>f</m> through <m>P_0(1,1)</m>,
while the directions of most rapid increase and decrease are
perpendicular to it.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-gradient-directions-tikz">
<caption>
The level curves of <m>f(x,y) = \frac{x^2}{2} + \frac{y^2}{2}</m> are
circles centered at the origin. At <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, the direction of
fastest increase points along <m>\nabla f</m>, the direction of fastest
decrease points along <m>-\nabla f</m>, and the two directions of zero
change are tangent to the level curve through <m>P_0</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-gradient-directions-tikz" width="70%">
<shortdescription>
Circular level curves with the four special directions marked at the
point one comma one.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Concentric circles, the level curves of
<m>f(x,y) = x^2/2 + y^2/2</m>, centered at the origin. At the point
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>, four unit arrows are drawn: a green arrow pointing
outward along the gradient (fastest increase), a vermillion arrow
pointing inward opposite to the gradient (fastest decrease), and two
dashed blue arrows tangent to the level circle through <m>P_0</m>
(zero change). A dashed tangent line to the circle at <m>P_0</m>
contains the two blue arrows.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.75, line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  % axes
  \draw[-{Latex}, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (-2.35,0) -- (2.5,0)
    node[below left] {$x$};
  \draw[-{Latex}, black!75, line width=0.7pt] (0,-2.35) -- (0,2.5)
    node[below left] {$y$};
  % level curves: x^2/2 + y^2/2 = c, radius sqrt(2c)
  \foreach \r in {0.707, 1, 1.732, 2}
    \draw[black!30] (0,0) circle (\r);
  % highlighted level curve through P0: c = 1, radius sqrt(2)
  \draw[oiblue!60, line width=1.1pt] (0,0) circle (1.41421);
  \node[black!60, font=\scriptsize, anchor=north east] at (-0.35,-0.62)
    {$f=\tfrac14$};
  \node[black!60, font=\scriptsize, anchor=north east] at (-1.2,-1.28)
    {$f=1$};
  \node[black!60, font=\scriptsize, anchor=north east] at (-1.48,-1.58)
    {$f=\tfrac32$};
  % tangent line to level curve at P0 (direction (1,-1))
  \draw[black!45, dashed] (0.15,1.85) -- (1.85,0.15);
  % the four directions at P0 (unit arrows, drawn at scale 0.8)
  % fastest increase: (1/sqrt2, 1/sqrt2)
  \draw[-{Latex}, oigreen, line width=1.6pt] (1,1) -- (1.566,1.566)
    node[above right=-2pt, font=\small]
    {$\dfrac{\nabla f}{|\nabla f|}$};
  % fastest decrease: -(1/sqrt2, 1/sqrt2)
  \draw[-{Latex}, oivermillion, line width=1.6pt] (1,1) -- (0.434,0.434)
    node[below left=-2pt, font=\small]
    {$-\dfrac{\nabla f}{|\nabla f|}$};
  % zero change: (1/sqrt2, -1/sqrt2) and (-1/sqrt2, 1/sqrt2)
  \draw[-{Latex}, oiblue, line width=1.4pt, densely dashed]
    (1,1) -- (1.566,0.434)
    node[below right=-2pt, font=\small] {$\mathbf u$};
  \draw[-{Latex}, oiblue, line width=1.4pt, densely dashed]
    (1,1) -- (0.434,1.566)
    node[above left=-2pt, font=\small] {$\tilde{\mathbf u}$};
  % base point
  \fill (1,1) circle (1.3pt) node[right=3pt, font=\small] {$P_0(1,1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-gradient-directions-prefigure">
<caption>
At <m>P_0(1,1)</m>: fastest increase along
<m>\nabla f/|\nabla f|</m>, fastest decrease along
<m>-\nabla f/|\nabla f|</m>, and zero change along
<m>\pm\mathbf u</m>, tangent to the level curve <m>f = 1</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-gradient-directions">
<diagram dimensions="(320,320)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-2.5,-2.5,2.7,2.7)">
<axes at="gd-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<circle at="gd-level1" center="(0,0)" radius="0.707" stroke="gray"
        fill="none"/>
<circle at="gd-level2" center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="gray"
        fill="none"/>
<circle at="gd-level3" center="(0,0)" radius="1.414" stroke="blue"
        thickness="2" fill="none"/>
<circle at="gd-level4" center="(0,0)" radius="1.732" stroke="gray"
        fill="none"/>
<line at="gd-tangent" endpoints="((0.15,1.85),(1.85,0.15))" stroke="gray"
      dash="6 4" thickness="1.5"/>
<vector at="gd-increase" v="(0.566,0.566)" tail="(1,1)" stroke="green"
        thickness="3"/>
<vector at="gd-decrease" v="(-0.566,-0.566)" tail="(1,1)" stroke="red"
        thickness="3"/>
<vector at="gd-zero1" v="(0.566,-0.566)" tail="(1,1)" stroke="blue"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="gd-zero2" v="(-0.566,0.566)" tail="(1,1)" stroke="blue"
        thickness="2"/>
<point at="gd-P0" p="(1,1)" fill="black" alignment="e">
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>
</point>
<label at="gd-increase-label" anchor="(1.7,1.75)" alignment="ne"
       color="green">
<m>\nabla f/|\nabla f|</m>
</label>
<label at="gd-decrease-label" anchor="(0.38,0.28)" alignment="sw"
       color="red">
<m>-\nabla f/|\nabla f|</m>
</label>
<label at="gd-zero1-label" anchor="(1.68,0.38)" alignment="se"
       color="blue">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="gd-zero2-label" anchor="(0.38,1.68)" alignment="nw"
       color="blue">
<m>\tilde{\mathbf u}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Concentric circles centered at the origin, the level curves of f of x comma y equals x squared over 2 plus y squared over 2. At the point P 0 with coordinates 1 comma 1, four unit arrows are drawn: a green arrow pointing outward along the gradient, the direction of fastest increase; a red arrow pointing inward opposite the gradient, the direction of fastest decrease; and two blue arrows tangent to the level circle through P 0, the directions of zero change. A dashed line tangent to the circle at P 0 contains the two blue arrows.">
  <annotation ref="gd-level3" text="The level curve f equals 1 passing through the point P 0, a circle of radius root 2, highlighted in blue."/>
  <annotation ref="gd-P0" text="The point P 0 with coordinates 1 comma 1, where the four special directions are drawn."/>
  <annotation ref="gd-increase" text="The green unit vector 1 over root 2 comma 1 over root 2, the direction of the gradient, in which f increases most rapidly."/>
  <annotation ref="gd-decrease" text="The red unit vector negative 1 over root 2 comma negative 1 over root 2, opposite to the gradient, in which f decreases most rapidly."/>
  <annotation ref="gd-zero1" text="The blue unit vector u equals 1 over root 2 comma negative 1 over root 2, a direction of zero change, tangent to the level curve."/>
  <annotation ref="gd-zero2" text="The blue unit vector u tilde equals negative u, the other direction of zero change, also tangent to the level curve."/>
  <annotation ref="gd-tangent" text="The dashed tangent line to the level curve at P 0, which contains both directions of zero change."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<interactive xml:id="interactive-gradient-max-directions" iframe="gradient-max-directions.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the unit vector <m>\mathbf u</m> around the point <m>P_0(1,1)</m>
on the level-curve plot of
<m>f(x,y) = \frac{x^2}{2} + \frac{y^2}{2}</m> and watch the value of
<m>D_{\mathbf u} f = u_1 + u_2</m>. Use the buttons to snap
<m>\mathbf u</m> to the answers of Example III: the direction of
fastest increase, the direction of fastest decrease, and the two
directions of no change.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>

<interactive xml:id="interactive-gradient-max-directions-3d" iframe="gradient-max-directions-3d.html" width="100%" aspect="8:6">
<instructions>
<p>
A 3D view of Example III. The surface
<m>z = \frac{x^2}{2} + \frac{y^2}{2}</m> is shown with the level curve
<m>f = 1</m>, the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2</m>, passing through the point
<m>(1,1,1)</m>; its dashed projection lies in the <m>xy</m>-plane. Drag
the slider to rotate the direction
<m>\mathbf u = \langle\cos\varphi, \sin\varphi\rangle</m> at
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>: the vertical plane in the direction <m>\mathbf u</m>
cuts the surface in a curve whose tangent line at <m>(1,1,1)</m> has
slope <m>D_{\mathbf u} f = \cos\varphi + \sin\varphi</m>. Use the
buttons to snap to the answers of Example III, and note that when
<m>D_{\mathbf u} f = 0</m>, the vector <m>\mathbf u</m> is tangent to
the level curve. Drag the figure to view it from a different angle, or
press the <q>Rotate</q> button.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>

</subsection>

</section>
